@page "/HowItWorks/partial-imports"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/partial-imports">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                The import page has a "Load" tick box for each type of event: stocks, funds, options, futures, FX,
                dividends and interest income. Unticking one leaves that type out of the files you import. Many events
                depend on each other, though. Excess reportable income (ERI) and equalisation change the cost of a fund
                holding, equalisation reduces a dividend, and an exercised option changes the cost of the shares
                delivered. Leaving one side out changes the other side's result. This page explains what happens in each
                case, and what the app does about it.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">What the tick boxes filter</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The tick boxes apply to Interactive Brokers XML files and saved session (JSON) files. FreeTrade,
                    Trading212 and Interactive Investor CSV files always load their trades, dividends and interest,
                    whatever is ticked.
                </li>
                <li>
                    They apply when a file is imported. Unticking a box later does not remove events already imported, and
                    each file you import is added to what is already there.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Corporate actions in a saved session are always loaded. These are stock splits, takeovers, spin-offs,
                    returns of capital, transfers with a spouse or civil partner, ERI and equalisation. From an Interactive
                    Brokers file, stock splits are always loaded and returns of capital only with "Load Dividends".
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">What happens when one side is missing</h2>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">You import</th>
                            <th scope="col">But not</th>
                            <th scope="col">What happens</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Fund trades</td>
                            <td>ERI</td>
                            <td>
                                Brokers do not report ERI, so a broker file on its own never has it. The ERI income is missing
                                from your income, and the cost of the fund is not raised, so the gain when you sell is too high
                                by the same amount. The app cannot tell that ERI is missing.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>ERI or equalisation</td>
                            <td>The fund's trades</td>
                            <td>
                                The ERI is still taxed as income: the amount was worked out from your holding when you entered
                                it. With no units in the Section 104 pool to adjust, the cost change goes into an empty pool,
                                and the next purchase of the fund in the imported trades picks that cost up.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>ERI</td>
                            <td>Dividends or interest (box unticked)</td>
                            <td>
                                ERI income is always counted, whatever is ticked, because ERI also changes the fund's cost.
                                Unticking "Load Dividends" or "Load Interest Income" leaves out only broker-reported income.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Equalisation on a dividend or interest payment</td>
                            <td>That dividend or interest</td>
                            <td>
                                An equalisation entry only exists with the income it reduces. If that income is not imported,
                                the entry is left out and a warning lists it. The cost reduction it made to the fund is lost
                                too, so enter it again once the income is there. Equalisation on an ERI entry stays, because
                                ERI is always loaded.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Options</td>
                            <td>Stocks</td>
                            <td>
                                An option that was exercised or assigned needs the share trade that delivered the shares. If it
                                is missing, the import stops with a "No corresponding trade found" error.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Stocks</td>
                            <td>Options</td>
                            <td>
                                Shares bought or sold when an option was exercised are taken at the strike price only. The
                                option premium, which would normally be added to the cost or proceeds, is left out.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Anything</td>
                            <td>FX or futures</td>
                            <td>
                                FX and futures stand on their own. Leaving them out removes their gains and losses and does not
                                change anything else.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example: fund trades without ERI</h2>
                <p>
                    You hold 1,000 units of an Irish ETF costing £10,000. Its reporting period ends on 31 December 2023 with
                    ERI of £0.50 a unit, and on 1 September 2024 you sell all 1,000 units for £12,000.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>With the ERI entered:</strong> £500 of dividend income in 2024/25, and the pool cost becomes
                        £10,500 on the distribution date, 30 June 2024. The gain on the sale is £12,000 − £10,500 =
                        <strong>£1,500</strong>.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Broker file only:</strong> no ERI income, and a gain of £12,000 − £10,000 =
                        <strong>£2,000</strong>. Your income is £500 too low and your gain £500 too high.
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    If you imported the saved session with "Load Stocks" and "Load Funds" unticked instead, the £500 is still
                    taxed as income. The pool shows £500 of cost against 0 units.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Getting a complete result</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    To pick up where you left off, import the saved session with every box ticked. It holds your broker
                    events together with everything entered by hand, so every link between them is restored.
                </li>
                <li>
                    If you start again from broker files, tick every box, then enter ERI, equalisation and other corporate
                    actions again. Equalisation is linked to a particular imported income, so it cannot be carried over from
                    a separate session onto freshly imported broker dividends.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Untick a box only to leave a type out for good, for example futures you report elsewhere. Do not untick
                    one to split an import into parts.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Read the warnings shown after each import. They list any equalisation entries left out.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/reporting-funds" class="link-styled">Excess reportable income and equalisation</a></li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a></li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/options" class="link-styled">Options</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
